7.8.m:
Engaged in yoga, the two deities, assuming their supreme forms, / the sources of the entire world, Pārvatī and the lord of the world, / full of great splendour, remained for a very long time for the sake of a son. / That cannot be described by me, nor by Brahmā, O great sage: / that great union of the goddess and the god of gods who holds the trident. / He, the supreme lord, is higher than Brahmā and those like me. / Yet it must certainly be told according to the tradition, as it occurred and as it was heard; / therefore I will tell it to you, Vyāsa, for you are my dear disciple.
[…] / While they remained thus, O great sage, engaged in union through yoga, / a thousand divine years passed by.
Then there arose terrible and terrifying omens: / the entire earth, with its mountains, woods, and forests, trembled; / the rivers then went dry; the ocean was agitated; / the sun lost its brilliance; a hot wind blew;

so that no son born of the goddess's body will come to be, / [and] Śambhu will not produce a son harmful to us. / We ourselves are all the elder sons of the all-pervading Śaṅkara; / he cannot be seen through austerity nor through knowledge, O lords of gods;
that bull-bannered [god] can be seen only through supreme devotion. / Come, let us go where Śaṅkara abides, / so that, relying on supreme devotion, I may behold Śaṅkara. / And the god Fire, consumer of oblations, shall enter into his presence,
carrying the petition of all the gods, O best of gods.' / Having heard this speech of Viṣṇu of immeasurable splendor,

