Horses and other [mounts] did not neigh, and the elephants became depressed;
from a cloudless sky fell a great quantity of blood, terrifying to all.
Weapons blazed forth with smoke, O son of Śakti;
headless trunks appeared in the sun, and a battle was seen there.
[The battle] was between two men, O Vyāsa, and wailing arose on all sides;
distress seized the gods, and anxiety overcame their minds.
Left eyes twitched, and yawning arose;
birds shrieked in an omen-bearing, terrifying manner;
and fire blazed there with sound and harshness.
Seeing these and other various portents signifying danger,
Śakra spoke these words to the preceptor Bṛhaspati:
"O venerable one, what is this dreadful fear that has come upon us,
which these fierce and very terrible portents announce to us?
I think these are not without a cause, nor is it a small [danger] for us,
since these fierce and terrible [signs] portend like the end of an aeon."

Hearing those words of his, Bṛhaspati, the preceptor of the gods,
observed, considered with his intellect, and then spoke these words:
"What you say is true; these are indeed not without a cause;
there will be a very great cause here, without a doubt.
The original cause was the foremost daitya king, Hiraṇyakaśipu, in the past;
he was slain by Narasiṃha, and the kingdom was attained by us once again.
For twelve thousand years up to this day, completely at ease,
all the worlds have been enjoyed by you, the great-souled ones.
The lords of the daityas, devoid of energy, have been lying down together with the dānavas;
and the sons of Danu [dwell] in Pātāla, afflicted with misery and grief."
